{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgVZHBXtgPmMhSwHepKN8","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_012mRFWGmHZRQysETNivmVpZ","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /workspace/paper; sed -n '/section{Method/,$p' paper.tex | grep -v '^% @evidence' | grep -n -i -E 'cite|known|prior|literature|standard|typically|commonly|usually|widely|previous|recent|integer|NP|combinator|greedy|knapsack|Newton|swap|exact|sports|NBA|LLM judge|annotator|noise' | head -80"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":5120,"cache_read_input_tokens":27625,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":5120},"output_tokens":7,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"7a74eef6-ea20-4747-bb70-aa84f50546b5","uuid":"e0052c08-14a6-4bc1-8c4c-88e9e2da0cea","timestamp":"2026-10-29T00:09:53.327Z","request_id":"req_011CgVZHB9okaUyVyvDGWpJf"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_012mRFWGmHZRQysETNivmVpZ","type":"tool_result","content":"5:Let $L$ and $C$ be the $k$-th and $(k{+}1)$-th players under $\\hat\\theta$; for top-1 these are the leader and the runner-up. Let $m=\\hat\\theta_L-\\hat\\theta_C>0$ be the decisive margin and $c=e_L-e_C$. The negative Hessian of \\eqref{eq:bt} at $\\hat\\theta$ is $H=\\sum_r p_r(1-p_r)(e_{a_r}-e_{b_r})(e_{a_r}-e_{b_r})^\\top+\\lambda I$, with $p_r=\\sigma(\\hat\\theta_{a_r}-\\hat\\theta_{b_r})$. Row $r$ contributes the gradient $(y_r-p_r)(e_{a_r}-e_{b_r})$. Removing it moves the optimum by approximately $-H^{-1}(y_r-p_r)(e_{a_r}-e_{b_r})$~\\citep{Cook2000DetectionOI,Koh2017UnderstandingBP}. With $v=H^{-1}c$, the first-order effects of dropping or flipping row $r$ on the margin are therefore\n13:\\paragraph{Exact verification by prefix search.}\n14:The linear approximation only orders rows; it never sets the reported size. For a prefix $D_s$ of length $s$ we refit \\eqref{eq:bt} exactly. The refit is a damped Newton method on the perturbed sufficient statistics, warm-started at $\\hat\\theta$ and stopped at a gradient norm below $10^{-10}$, and the target condition is tested on the result. The search doubles $s=1,2,4,\\dots$ until a prefix changes the target, then bisects between the last failing and the first succeeding length. This requires $O(\\log \\kver)$ refits. The search returns the shortest changing prefix it locates, and the evaluator re-verifies the returned set with its own independent refit. Because the refit after a perturbation is not guaranteed to be monotone in $s$, bisection may miss a shorter changing prefix. Even then, the returned set is a valid certificate. The finder is a single NumPy module (Appendix~\\ref{app:repro}). It is deterministic: its seed only initializes a random generator that the search never uses.\n17:$\\kver$ is an \\emph{upper bound} on $k^\\star$: an explicit set of $\\kver$ rows exists whose perturbation changes the target under the exact estimator. Nothing smaller is ruled out. Computing $k^\\star$ is a combinatorial search problem. Provable auditing is known only in special settings such as low-dimensional least squares~\\citep{Moitra2022ProvablyAO}. We do not claim to solve it on real data. Instead, we check on a planted case with a known exact minimum that the instrument is not loose (below).\n20:Two comparators are run by the evaluator on identical data. \\emph{Random:} the smallest $s$ such that at least 10 of 20 uniformly random size-$s$ subsets change the target. The search uses the same doubling and bisection and is capped at half the rows. This is a noise reference: the size at which a \\emph{typical} perturbation, not a chosen one, flips the conclusion. \\emph{Direct heuristic:} the ``obvious attack''. It first removes the leader's wins over the challenger, then the leader's other wins in order of decreasing opponent strength. We summarize each comparator per case as $\\mathrm{clip}(\\log_2(k_{\\text{cmp}}/\\kver),-4,4)$, average it over seeds, then over the 15 real cases. A 95\\% percentile-bootstrap interval over cases uses 2{,}000 resamples. Comparator definitions are in \\nolinkurl{task/evaluate_final.py}, lines 578--640.\n23:\\emph{Planted minimum:} a synthetic three-player case with a near-tied leader pair. Its exact minimal drop size is computed by brute-force enumeration over per-pair winner counts, which are sufficient statistics for \\eqref{eq:bt}. \\emph{Row permutation:} the Arena drop/top-1 case with its rows presented in a different order. A correct instrument must return the same size. \\emph{Seed replay:} each case runs under three seeds, and one episode is replayed and its output hash compared. Before any search, the evaluator also checks each case. It rejects cases whose decisive margin is degenerate ($m\\le 10\\tau$). It requires that the empty perturbation leaves the target unchanged, and that an independent refit on reversed row order reproduces $\\hat\\theta$ (lines 643--654).\n26:Table~\\ref{tab:cases} lists the cases. The four designs are crowdsourced votes from the Chatbot Arena 140k human-preference release~\\citep{Chiang2024ChatbotAA}; expert human and GPT-4 pairwise judgments of MT-Bench answers~\\citep{Zheng2023JudgingLW}; and NBA games from two seasons of the FiveThirtyEight Elo record, the 2014 and 2015 seasons in that file's labeling, with each game a comparison between two teams. Arena ``tie'' and ``both bad'' votes and MT-Bench tie judgments become ties ($y=\\tfrac12$); NBA games have no ties. Each design contributes three cases: drop/top-1, flip/top-1, and drop/top-$k$. With the two controls this gives 17 cases. All method choices were made on disjoint development data: the Arena 55k release and the NBA 1983 season. Any final-split row whose content hash also appears there is excluded; the recorded cross-split overlap is zero. The 17 cases were then run once in a sealed evaluation under seeds 101, 102, and 103. Every episode either returns a set that the evaluator verifies, or counts as a failure; none failed. The evaluation completed and passed all of its validity gates.\n30:\\caption{Evaluation designs and cases. $n$ is the number of comparison rows after filtering, recovered exactly from the evaluator's reported fraction $\\kver/n$ and the verified size $\\kver$ (Appendix~\\ref{app:derivation}). Every source contributes drop/top-1, flip/top-1, and drop/top-$k$ cases.}\n39:LLM judge & MT-Bench GPT-4 pair judgments & 2{,}400 & 3 & one GPT-4 verdict \\\\\n40:Sports & NBA, 2014 season (FiveThirtyEight) & 1{,}319 & 5 & one game \\\\\n41:Sports & NBA, 2015 season (FiveThirtyEight) & 1{,}311 & 5 & one game \\\\\n52:\\subsection{The instrument is exact on a planted case, order-invariant, and deterministic}\n55:Table~\\ref{tab:validity} collects the evidence that the measured sizes mean what we claim. On the planted synthetic case, the verified drop size is 8 and the control score is 0. This score is $\\min(0,\\log_2(k^\\star/\\kver))$, so a value of 0 means the instrument returned exactly the brute-force minimum. Here, at least, the upper bound is tight. Under row permutation, the Arena drop/top-1 size is 62 in both presentations (control score 0), so the result does not depend on row order. All 15 real-case sizes are identical across seeds 101, 102, and 103. The replayed episode reproduced its output hash (replay match 1.0), and no episode failed. Since the finder is deterministic, seed agreement shows reproducibility. It does \\emph{not} measure sampling uncertainty (\\S\\ref{sec:limitations}).\n57:Against the random comparator, the influence-ordered sets are far smaller. The mean clipped $\\log_2(k_{\\text{rand}}/\\kver)$ is 3.975, with 95\\% CI [3.934, 4.0]. This corresponds to a geometric-mean factor of about $15.7\\times$. The upper end of the interval sits at the clip value of 4, so most cases are censored, and the true advantage is \\emph{at least} this large. Fragility to chosen perturbations is therefore a different quantity from fragility to noise. A leader that survives typical random corruption of tens of comparisons can still fall to a targeted set of the same size.\n59:Against the direct heuristic, the influence ordering was never worse in the Arena, GPT-4-judge, and NBA families: each family score is 0. In the MT-Bench-expert family, the score of $-0.0053$ shows that the heuristic was marginally smaller in at least one case. The evaluator records this only at family level. The ordering therefore improves on the obvious ``remove the leader's wins'' attack in most cases, but not uniformly.\n62:The configuration evaluated here \\emph{is} the influence ordering plus verified prefix search. Its sizes coincide with those of the evaluator's own one-step-influence comparator in every case: the mean $\\log_2$ ratio is 0.0, with CI [0.0, 0.0]. The top-1 drop fractions are equal to the reported precision. The pre-declared test of a strict improvement over this ordering (CI lower bound $>0$) is therefore \\emph{not supported}. We make no claim of algorithmic superiority. Refinements that relinearize the influence after partial removals, or that prune and swap rows within the verified set, were designed but never produced scored evaluations. Their effect is unknown, not negative (Appendix~\\ref{app:negative}).\n81:vs.\\ direct heuristic (family scores) & Arena $0$, MTB-GPT-4 $0$, NBA $0$ & never worse \\\\\n93:\\caption{Verified perturbation sizes for all 15 real cases, on two scales (log axes). (a) Count $\\kver$ of comparisons dropped or flipped; integers are annotated. (b) The same sizes as a percentage of the source's comparisons, $\\kver/n$. Colors mark the target and perturbation; top-$k$ is top-5 for Arena and NBA and top-3 for MT-Bench (MTB). Sizes are identical across the three seeds.}\n97:Figure~\\ref{fig:main} shows the main result. The leader changes after dropping 62 Arena votes, 92 expert judgments, 143 GPT-4 judgments, 7 games of the 2014 NBA season, or 16 games of the 2015 season. Flipping requires 46, 46, 61, 4, and 8 rows, respectively. As fractions, the drop sizes are 0.046\\% of Arena, 2.74\\% of MT-Bench expert judgments, 5.96\\% of GPT-4 judgments, and 0.53\\% and 1.22\\% of the two NBA seasons. The NBA fractions average 0.876\\%.\n105:In all five sources, the verified flip size for top-1 is smaller than the drop size. The ratio of drop to flip size is 1.35 for Arena, 2.0 for MT-Bench experts, 2.34 for the GPT-4 judge, 1.75 for NBA 2014, and 2.0 for NBA 2015. This matches the first-order prediction of Eq.~\\eqref{eq:influence}. Reversing a verdict both removes evidence for the leader and adds evidence against it, so its effect exceeds that of a drop by the factor $1/(1-p_r)$, which is near 2 for close match-ups. Arena's ratio, the furthest from 2, is not explained by our data. Ties can be dropped but not flipped, and the approximation ignores refitting, so we do not over-interpret it. For manipulation threat models, the flip size is the relevant one: an actor who controls a vote controls its direction.\n108:Moving the top-5 set needs 21 Arena votes, against 62 for the leader. In \\emph{both} NBA seasons, a single game suffices. In MT-Bench the top-3 set costs more than the leader: 229 vs.\\ 92 expert judgments and 150 vs.\\ 143 GPT-4 judgments. The cost is governed by the gap at the boundary being tested, not by the leader's lead. In the NBA seasons, the 5th and 6th teams are close enough that one game reorders them. The evaluator's degenerate-margin check rules out exact ties ($m\\le 10\\tau$). So ``is the top-$k$ stable?'' and ``is the leader stable?'' are different questions, and a leaderboard can pass one and fail the other.\n113:\\caption{Controlled contrasts. (a) MT-Bench: the same question set judged by expert humans (filled circles) and by GPT-4 (open squares). Values are $\\kver/n$ in percent, with counts annotated. The GPT-4 leader needs a larger share of its data to change for top-1, but a smaller one for the top-3 set. (b) Fragility rank of the drop/top-1 cases by count (left, with $\\kver$) and by fraction (right, with $\\kver/n$). Rank 1 (top) is the most fragile. The ordering of Arena and the NBA seasons reverses between scales (purple).}\n117:\\paragraph{Expert humans vs.\\ an LLM judge on the same questions.}\n121:Figure~\\ref{fig:contrasts}b orders the five drop/top-1 cases by count and by fraction. By count, the two NBA seasons are the most fragile (7, 16), followed by Arena (62). By fraction, Arena moves to first place (0.0457\\%), ahead of NBA 2014 (0.531\\%) and NBA 2015 (1.22\\%). The two MT-Bench sources stay last on both scales. A cross-benchmark statement such as ``crowdsourced leaderboards are more fragile than sports tables'' is therefore true on one natural scale and false on the other. Any such comparison must name its scale and justify it by the threat model (\\S\\ref{sec:setup}).\n127:For one fixed and widely used estimator, the following holds across four evaluation designs. Explicit, verified sets of tens of comparisons change the leader. Typical random perturbations need roughly 16 times as many rows. The relative fragility of designs depends on whether size is counted or proportioned, and on whether the conclusion is about the leader or a top-$k$ set. The protocol is cheap. All 17 cases, including the 135,622-row Arena case, completed in about 16\\,s of recorded episode time per seed (15.5--16.3\\,s). This covers data transfer, the search, and the evaluator's verification refit, and it is possible because each refit acts on $p\\times p$ sufficient statistics. For practitioners, this suggests reporting a verified drop and flip size alongside resampling intervals, for both the leader and any advertised top-$k$ claim.\n130:Every size is an upper bound $\\kver\\ge k^\\star$. Exactness is demonstrated only on the three-player planted case. On real data the true minimum may be smaller, possibly by a lot for cases with many players. Statements such as ``the leader rests on 62 votes'' should be read as ``at most 62 votes''.\n136:All results use ridge BT with $\\lambda=0.1$, ties counted as half wins, and a 50-comparison minimum per player. Other choices could change sizes, sometimes qualitatively. Examples include Elo-style online updates~\\citep{Boubdir2023EloUR}, a Davidson tie model~\\citep{Davidson1970OnET}, a different penalty, or style-controlled scores. Each design is represented by a single snapshot, and sports by two seasons. We have no within-design replication across releases.\n142:The direct heuristic is reported only at family level, so per-case heuristic sizes are unknown. We evaluated one search configuration, the influence ordering with verified prefix search. Iterative relinearization and set-refinement variants were not evaluated, so whether they find materially smaller sets remains open. Our sizes are thus conservative descriptions of fragility, not tight ones.\n147:We measured how many comparisons must be dropped or reversed to change a Bradley--Terry leaderboard. The instrument orders comparisons by their influence on the decisive margin and certifies each size by exact refits; on a planted case it recovers the true minimum. Across crowdsourced votes, expert and LLM-judge annotations, and professional sports, tens of comparisons suffice to change the leader. These sets are roughly sixteen times smaller than random perturbations: fragility to chosen data differs from fragility to noise. Cross-design comparisons are scale- and target-dependent. The Arena leader is the most fragile as a share of its data but not as a count. NBA top-5 sets move with one game. MT-Bench top-3 sets resist more than their leaders. Expert and GPT-4 judges trade places between targets. We therefore recommend reporting verified fragility on both scales and for each advertised target. Such reports should be read as upper bounds on a fixed estimator, not as minima or causal properties of an evaluation design. Tighter certificates, data-resampling uncertainty for $\\kver$, and controlled within-design replications are the natural next steps.\n183:NBA 2014 & drop, top-1 & 7 & 1{,}319 & 0.531 \\\\\n186:NBA 2015 & drop, top-1 & 16 & 1{,}311 & 1.22 \\\\\n199:The evaluation record reports, for each family, the top-1 drop and flip fractions $\\kver/n$, averaged over seeds and, for NBA, over the two seasons. For Arena and the two MT-Bench sources, $n=\\kver_{\\text{drop}}/(\\kver/n)_{\\text{drop}}$; this yields 135,622, 3,355, and 2,400 to within floating-point error. For NBA, the season-mean drop fraction $\\tfrac12(7/n_{14}+16/n_{15})=0.8756\\%$ and flip fraction $\\tfrac12(4/n_{14}+8/n_{15})=0.4567\\%$ form a $2\\times2$ linear system in $(1/n_{14},1/n_{15})$. Its solution gives $n_{14}=1{,}319$ and $n_{15}=1{,}311$. The two seasons sum to 2,630 games, matching the row count of the season file in the final data split. The script \\nolinkurl{analysis/derive_stats.py} performs this derivation from \\texttt{paper\\_data.json} and writes \\nolinkurl{analysis/derived_stats.json}.\n205:For each real source, the evaluator loads the pinned resource and drops rows shared with the development split. It then shuffles the rows with a fixed private seed and iteratively removes players with fewer than 50 comparisons. Finally, it relabels players with a random permutation, so the finder sees only integer identifiers and outcomes, never names, text, or dates (\\nolinkurl{task/evaluate_final.py}, lines 746--831). The original top-$k$ set, the decisive pair, and the margin come from the evaluator's own fit.\n214:Three players meet in three pairs of 50--80 games each. One pair is nearly balanced, with one to three wins above an even split; the other pairs have win rates between 0.55 and 0.70. Before the case is accepted, its exact minimal drop size is found by enumerating all compositions of per-pair winner classes up to size 8. Cases with an exact minimum below 2 are rejected (lines 658--721). The finder returned a size-8 set, equal to the enumerated minimum.\n224:Three refined search configurations were designed to target that improvement. All three relinearize the influence after partial removals and exploit the fact that \\eqref{eq:bt} depends only on per-pair counts. The first adds multi-pair targeting and exact pruning. The second adds deterministic refit budgets and a two-for-one swap refinement. The third adds exact pruning seeded from the influence set. None produced a scored evaluation: all three of three were unscored because execution failed before evaluation. Their effect on $\\kver$ is unknown and should not be read as a loss. No result in the main text depends on them.\n230:All data are public. Arena: \\nolinkurl{lmarena-ai/arena-human-preference-140k} at commit \\texttt{6322995ab34d}, CC-BY-4.0. MT-Bench: \\nolinkurl{lmsys/mt_bench_human_judgments} at commit \\texttt{f7d2896d2cc5}, human and GPT-4-pair files, CC-BY-4.0. NBA: \\nolinkurl{fivethirtyeight/data} at commit \\texttt{4c1ff5e3aef1}, file \\nolinkurl{nba-elo/nbaallelo.csv}, CC-BY-4.0. Development used the Arena 55k release (commit \\texttt{18c298340948}) and the NBA 1983 season. Raw-file checksums and the content-hash split are recorded in \\nolinkurl{task/resources/final/split_manifest.json}.\n241:Large language models were used extensively and are disclosed here. An autonomous LLM-based research agent proposed the study design within the task's constraints. It implemented the perturbation finder and the refined configurations, wrote the analysis and plotting scripts, and drafted this manuscript, including its framing and text. Every number in the paper comes from the sealed evaluation record produced by executing the code, and the derivation scripts are included. Bibliographic metadata was retrieved from the Semantic Scholar API. Huang et al.~(2025) is cited by title only, as part of the provided background reading list. No result or method in this paper is taken from it. No paid API or GPU was used for the experiments.","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"7a74eef6-ea20-4747-bb70-aa84f50546b5","uuid":"ca7f967b-0a9b-46a4-8732-a4ba09072569","timestamp":"2026-10-29T00:09:53.415Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"5:Let $L$ and $C$ be the $k$-th and $(k{+}1)$-th players under $\\hat\\theta$; for top-1 these are the leader and the runner-up. Let $m=\\hat\\theta_L-\\hat\\theta_C>0$ be the decisive margin and $c=e_L-e_C$. The negative Hessian of \\eqref{eq:bt} at $\\hat\\theta$ is $H=\\sum_r p_r(1-p_r)(e_{a_r}-e_{b_r})(e_{a_r}-e_{b_r})^\\top+\\lambda I$, with $p_r=\\sigma(\\hat\\theta_{a_r}-\\hat\\theta_{b_r})$. Row $r$ contributes the gradient $(y_r-p_r)(e_{a_r}-e_{b_r})$. Removing it moves the optimum by approximately $-H^{-1}(y_r-p_r)(e_{a_r}-e_{b_r})$~\\citep{Cook2000DetectionOI,Koh2017UnderstandingBP}. With $v=H^{-1}c$, the first-order effects of dropping or flipping row $r$ on the margin