{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01TtihNgAHdXDDT5AYGzuxwF","type":"tool_result","content":"\\paragraph{Expert humans vs.\\ an LLM judge on the same questions.}\nMT-Bench is the closest thing to a controlled comparison in our data: the same benchmark is judged by experts and by GPT-4 (Fig.~\\ref{fig:contrasts}a). For top-1, the GPT-4-judge leader is harder to dislodge, at 143 vs.\\ 92 drops (5.96\\% vs.\\ 2.74\\%) and 61 vs.\\ 46 flips. For the top-3 set the order reverses: 150 vs.\\ 229 drops (6.25\\% vs.\\ 6.83\\%). Neither annotation source is uniformly more robust. The two sources also differ in $n$ (2,400 vs.\\ 3,355), in tie rates, and possibly in which model leads, since player identities are hidden from the finder. The contrast is therefore not a causal estimate of a ``judge effect''.\n% @evidence[[analysis/derived_stats.json#cases]]\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.mtbench_gpt4_top1_flip_frac]]\n\n\\paragraph{Fragility rankings reverse between scales.}\n45:\\section{Introduction}\n69:\\section{Related Work}\n81:\\section{Problem Setup}\n101:\\section{Method: Refit-Verified Influence Ordering}\n152:\\section{Results}\n155:\\subsection{The instrument is exact on a planted case, order-invariant, and deterministic}\n201:\\subsection{Tens of comparisons change the leader in every design}\n219:\\subsection{Drop vs.\\ flip, top-1 vs.\\ top-$k$, and the scale reversal}\n246:\\section{Discussion and Limitations}\n268:\\section{Conclusion}\n281:\\section{Supplementary Information}\n284:\\subsection{Per-case verified sizes}\n323:\\subsection{Recovering $n$ from the evaluation record}\n330:\\subsection{Estimator, cases, comparators and controls in detail}\n350:\\subsection{Pre-declared superiority test and unevaluated refinements}\n359:\\subsection{Reproducibility record}\n371:\\subsection{LLM usage}\n['status', 'selected_prompt_family', 'recommended_title', 'central_question', 'central_thesis', 'narrative_rationale', 'prior_paper_story_status', 'edit_framing_decision', 'staged_input_impact', 'method_groups', 'results_focus', 'figure_plan', 'figure_specs', 'supplementary_plan', 'forbidden_headings', 'forbidden_phrases', 'notes_for_writer', 'failure_reason']","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"7a74eef6-ea20-4747-bb70-aa84f50546b5","uuid":"fbae5e9e-1a4b-41ef-afe5-c9975044941a","timestamp":"2026-10-29T00:10:19.952Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"\\paragraph{Expert humans vs.\\ an LLM judge on the same questions.}\nMT-Bench is the closest thing to a controlled comparison in our data: the same benchmark is judged by experts and by GPT-4 (Fig.~\\ref{fig:contrasts}a). For top-1, the GPT-4-judge leader is harder to dislodge, at 143 vs.\\ 92 drops (5.96\\% vs.\\ 2.74\\%) and 61 vs.\\ 46 flips. For the top-3 set the order reverses: 150 vs.\\ 229 drops (6.25\\% vs.\\ 6.83\\%). Neither annotation source is uniformly more robust. The two sources also differ in $n$ (2,400 vs.\\ 3,355), in tie rates, and possibly in which model leads, since player identities are hidden from the finder. The contrast is therefore not a causal estimate of a ``judge effect''.\n% @evidence[[analysis/derived_stats.json#cases]]\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.mtbench_gpt4_top1_flip_frac]]\n\n\\paragraph{Fragility rankings reverse between scales.}\n45:\\section{Introduction}\n69:\\section{Related Work}\n81:\\section{Problem Setup}\n101:\\section{Method: Refit-Verified Influence Ordering}\n152:\\section{Results}\n155:\\subsection{The instrument is exact on a planted case, order-invariant, and deterministic}\n201:\\subsection{Tens of comparisons change the leader in every design}\n219:\\subsection{Drop vs.\\ flip, top-1 vs.\\ top-$k$, and the scale reversal}\n246:\\section{Discussion and Limitations}\n268:\\section{Conclusion}\n281:\\section{Supplementary Information}\n284:\\subsection{Per-case verified sizes}\n323:\\subsection{Recovering $n$ from the evaluation record}\n330:\\subsection{Estimator, cases, comparators and controls in detail}\n350:\\subsection{Pre-declared superiority test and unevaluated refinements}\n359:\\subsection{Reproducibility record}\n371:\\subsection{LLM usage}\n['status', 'selected_prompt_family', 'recommended_title', 'central_question', 'central_thesis', 'narrative_rationale', 'prior_paper_story_status', 'edit_framing_decision', 'staged_input_impact', 'method_groups', 'results_focus', 'figure_plan', 'figure_specs', 'supplementary_plan', 'forbidden_headings', 'forbidden_phrases', 'notes_for_writer', 'failure_reason']","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false}}
