{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01TNmRFJNsSaonpLfJ9zbuYu","type":"tool_result","content":"\\section{Discussion and Limitations}\n\\label{sec:limitations}\n\n\\paragraph{What the evidence supports.}\nFor one fixed and widely used estimator, the following holds across four evaluation designs. Explicit, verified sets of tens of comparisons change the leader. Typical random perturbations need roughly 16 times as many rows. The relative fragility of designs depends on whether size is counted or proportioned, and on whether the conclusion is about the leader or a top-$k$ set. The protocol is cheap. All 17 cases, including the 135,622-row Arena case, completed in about 16\\,s of recorded episode time per seed (15.5--16.3\\,s). This covers data transfer, the search, and the evaluator's verification refit, and it is possible because each refit acts on $p\\times p$ sufficient statistics. For practitioners, this suggests reporting a verified drop and flip size alongside resampling intervals, for both the leader and any advertised top-$k$ claim.\n\n\\paragraph{Upper bounds, not minima.}\nEvery size is an upper bound $\\kver\\ge k^\\star$. Exactness is demonstrated only on the three-player planted case. On real data the true minimum may be smaller, possibly by a lot for cases with many players. Statements such as ``the leader rests on 62 votes'' should be read as ``at most 62 votes''.\n\n\\paragraph{No sampling uncertainty on $\\kver$.}\nThe three seeds give identical sizes because the finder is deterministic. They do not quantify how $\\kver$ would vary under resampling of comparisons or under a different data snapshot. The bootstrap intervals in Table~\\ref{tab:validity} are over cases, for comparator ratios, not over data. The random-comparator summary is censored at the clip of $\\log_2=4$.\n\n\\paragraph{One estimator, one snapshot per source.}\nAll results use ridge BT with $\\lambda=0.1$, ties counted as half wins, and a 50-comparison minimum per player. Other choices could change sizes, sometimes qualitatively. Examples include Elo-style online updates~\\citep{Boubdir2023EloUR}, a Davidson tie model~\\citep{Davidson1970OnET}, a different penalty, or style-controlled scores. Each design is represented by a single snapshot, and sports by two seasons. We have no within-design replication across releases.\n\n\\paragraph{Confounded cross-design differences.}\nThe designs differ in $n$, number of players, comparison-graph structure, tie rate, and the size of the decisive margin. With one case per design and target, we cannot attribute differences to the design itself, such as crowd versus judge. The cross-design statements are descriptive.\n\n\\paragraph{Comparator and search coverage.}\nThe direct heuristic is reported only at family level, so per-case heuristic sizes are unknown. We evaluated one search configuration, the influence ordering with verified prefix search. Iterative relinearization and set-refinement variants were not evaluated, so whether they find materially smaller sets remains open. Our sizes are thus conservative descriptions of fragility, not tight ones.\n\n<class 'dict'> ['campaign_results_dir', 'count', 'generated_at', 'queries', 'reference_query_plan', 'references', 'results_dir']\n# Reference Query Plan\n\n**Status:** ready\n\n## Central topic\nHow few comparisons must be **dropped** or **flipped** to change the **top-1** or **top-k set** of a Bradley-Terry leaderboard, compared across evaluation designs: crowdsourced votes (Chatbot Arena 140k), expert humans vs GPT-4 judges (MT-Bench), and sports (NBA 2014/2015). Synthetic and permutation controls are included.\n\n## What the run grounds\n- The final evaluated configuration is identical to the baseline (`source_evidence/downstream/program` == `baseline`). It is a one-step influence (AMIP-style) ranking of rows by their first-order effect on the leader-challenger margin. An exponential + binary prefix search with exact ridge-BT refits then verifies the result. Three seeds give identical verified sizes.\n- Verified sizes for the top-1 target (drop / flip): Arena-140k 62 / 46 (≈0.046% of rows dropped); MT-Bench human 92 / 46 (≈2.7%); MT-Bench GPT-4 143 / 61 (≈6.0%); NBA 2014 7 / 4; NBA 2015 16 / 8 (NBA ≈0.9%). Top-k sets are more fragile: Arena top-5 drop is 21, and NBA top-5 drop is 1.\n- Influence-guided sets are ≈16× smaller than random sets (mean log2 ratio ≈3.98, CI [3.93, 4.0]).\n- Three refined variants (category-count relinearized knapsack, swap refine, seeded prune) failed on harness infrastructure before they ran. They are **untested**, so no query treats them as a contribution.\n\n## Query set (9)\n| # | Category | Query | Why |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | core_method | approximate maximum influence perturbation dropping small fraction of data robustness | AMIP is the search behind every number; we also need follow-ups on exact/certified bounds to justify calling the sizes upper bounds |\n| 2 | core_method | influence functions infinitesimal jackknife approximate leave-one-out generalized linear models | grounds the one-step Newton/influence score for the logistic-type BT likelihood |\n| 3 | core_method | Bradley-Terry model maximum likelihood estimation existence MM algorithm paired comparisons | the estimator, its existence conditions under dropped rows, and how it is fitted |\n| 4 | baseline_comparator | top-K ranking from pairwise comparisons sample complexity spectral MLE | theory for top-k identifiability; ties to our top-k targets and the role of the margin |\n| 5 | baseline_comparator | adversarial manipulation of rank aggregation from pairwise comparisons | flip/drop sets are poisoning attacks; the closest comparator to vote rigging |\n| 6 | benchmark_context | Chatbot Arena crowdsourced pairwise human preference LLM evaluation leaderboard | context for the main crowdsourced dataset and its known critiques |\n| 7 | benchmark_context | LLM-as-a-judge agreement with human expert pairwise judgments biases | explains why human vs GPT-4 judges differ in fragility on MT-Bench |\n| 8 | benchmark_context | Elo rating predictive accuracy professional sports paired comparison | the sports-design control (NBA) |\n| 9 | result_driven_theme | confidence intervals uncertainty quantification Bradley-Terry ranking bootstrap | contrasts sampling CIs with worst-case data-dropping fragility |\n\n## Rejected alternatives and why\n- **Title-like queries** (\"leaderboard stability under data perturbation\", \"sensitivity of Bradley-Terry rankings\"). These risk surfacing the forbidden prior study or pages that cite it, so they are excluded by the task rules. The component-literature queries above cover the same ground without that risk.\n- **Generic queries** (\"robust optimization\", \"LLM benchmarking survey\", \"machine learning evaluation\"). They have low precision and no tie to influence, BT, or manipulation.\n- **Knapsack / Frank-Wolfe / relinearization queries.** These describe the untested variants and are not grounded by any evaluated result.\n- **Chronology / infra queries.** Excluded per the planning rules.\n\n## Forbidden patterns\nSee `forbidden_query_patterns` in the JSON: title copies of the research question, \"who cites / follow-ups of Huang et al.\" queries, generic filler, chronology or variant-name queries, and queries that present untested variants as validated.","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"7a74eef6-ea20-4747-bb70-aa84f50546b5","uuid":"ac1c97b1-8168-4c0d-b6d1-fc655d36305c","timestamp":"2026-10-29T00:10:29.683Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"\\section{Discussion and Limitations}\n\\label{sec:limitations}\n\n\\paragraph{What the evidence supports.}\nFor one fixed and widely used estimator, the following holds across four evaluation designs. Explicit, verified sets of tens of comparisons change the leader. Typical random perturbations need roughly 16 times as many rows. The relative fragility of desig