{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgVZNvFJFPG3dqhQdzNjM","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01FuhMjmbb3Uy6yVoVCGf5w1","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/workspace/paper/draft_reference_gaps.json","content":"{\n  \"status\": \"ready\",\n  \"summary\": \"Citation coverage is dense overall (all 27 bib entries cited), but three paragraph-specific gaps remain: the upper-bound/auditing positioning of the verified sizes, the corruption-robust ranking comparator family for flip perturbations, and benchmark context for the NBA sports design.\",\n  \"missing_literatures\": [\n    \"exact, greedy, and certified algorithms for worst-case data dropping and accuracy limits of one-step influence approximations\",\n    \"rank aggregation from pairwise comparisons under adversarial corruption or poisoning\",\n    \"paired-comparison and Bradley-Terry ratings of sports teams\"\n  ],\n  \"rewrite_targets\": [\n    {\n      \"target_id\": \"method-upper-bound-auditing\",\n      \"section\": \"method\",\n      \"priority\": \"high\",\n      \"citation_intent\": \"positioning\",\n      \"reason\": \"The paper's main interpretive caveat is that every reported size is an upper bound from a one-step influence ordering, and that the true minimum 'may be smaller, possibly by a lot'. The 'What a verified size means' paragraph supports this with a single citation (low-dimensional OLS auditing). The 'Upper bounds, not minima' limitation paragraph has no citation. Reviewers who know the drop-data literature will expect the follow-up work on algorithms that tighten or certify drop-data sets and on documented failure modes of the one-step approximation. That work bears directly on how loose the reported sizes might be.\",\n      \"anchor_text\": \"Computing $k^\\\\star$ is a combinatorial search problem. Provable auditing is known only in special settings ... (also Discussion: 'Upper bounds, not minima')\",\n      \"search_topics\": [\n        \"failure modes of approximate maximum influence perturbation for worst-case data dropping\",\n        \"greedy or exact algorithms for finding minimal data-dropping sets that overturn a conclusion\",\n        \"robustness auditing lower bounds certificates for small data subsets in regression\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"target_id\": \"related-work-corruption-robust-ranking\",\n      \"section\": \"related work\",\n      \"priority\": \"medium\",\n      \"citation_intent\": \"comparator\",\n      \"reason\": \"The flip perturbation is explicitly motivated as modeling 'mislabeled or adversarially cast votes'. The manipulation paragraph cites only one paper on attacks against rank aggregation (HodgeRank/spectral) plus LLM-specific vote rigging. The neighboring literature on ranking from pairwise comparisons when a fraction of the comparisons is adversarially corrupted is the natural theoretical comparator for the flip results, and it is not cited.\",\n      \"anchor_text\": \"Adversarial attacks on rank aggregation have also been analyzed as games between attacker and aggregator\",\n      \"search_topics\": [\n        \"rank aggregation from pairwise comparisons with adversarial corruptions\",\n        \"data poisoning attacks on Bradley-Terry or BTL ranking from pairwise comparisons\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"target_id\": \"method-data-sports-design\",\n      \"section\": \"method\",\n      \"priority\": \"medium\",\n      \"citation_intent\": \"benchmark_context\",\n      \"reason\": \"NBA seasons are one of the four evaluation designs and drive the headline count-scale finding (7 and 16 games; one game moves the top-5 set). The only sports citation is Elo's 1978 book on chess ratings. Nothing cites paired-comparison or BT modeling of team sports, so the sports design reads as an unmotivated add-on next to the well-cited LLM designs.\",\n      \"anchor_text\": \"NBA games from two seasons of the FiveThirtyEight Elo record ... each game a comparison between two teams\",\n      \"search_topics\": [\n        \"Bradley-Terry paired comparison models for rating sports teams\",\n        \"statistical rating systems for professional basketball or team sports\"\n      ]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"failure_reason\": \"\"\n}\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3141,"cache_read_input_tokens":45080,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":3141},"output_tokens":6,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"7a74eef6-ea20-4747-bb70-aa84f50546b5","uuid":"b80d7f6b-6930-4cce-af08-3587dfd49af9","timestamp":"2026-10-29T00:11:22.876Z","request_id":"req_011CgVZNuGW8iZXukty5tHqd"}
